## Hardware schematic and wiring

Below is a clear, buildable mapping. Use a 5V PSU rated 3A+, and power servos from a separate 5V rail with common ground to avoid brownouts.

### Pin mapping table

| Module | Signal | Raspberry Pi pin | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Camera 1 (user) | CSI/USB | CSI0 or USB | Choose CSI for lower latency |
| Camera 2 (bottle) | CSI/USB | CSI1 or USB | Maintain fixed focus on chute |
| Ultrasonic 1 (bottle detect) | TRIG/ECHO | GPIO 17 / GPIO 27 | Use 1 kΩ + 2 kΩ divider on ECHO to Pi |
| Ultrasonic 2 (bin status) | TRIG/ECHO | GPIO 22 / GPIO 23 | Same divider on ECHO |
| Buzzer | + / GND | GPIO 24 | Use NPN transistor + diode if >20 mA |
| Keypad (I2C) | SDA/SCL | GPIO 2 (SDA) / GPIO 3 (SCL) | 3.3V logic |
| LCD (I2C) | SDA/SCL | GPIO 2 (SDA) / GPIO 3 (SCL) | Shared I2C bus |
| Servo 1 (lid) | PWM | GPIO 18 (PWM) | External 5V rail, common GND |
| Servo 2 (routing) | PWM | GPIO 19 (PWM) | External 5V rail, common GND |
| Start button | BTN | GPIO 25 | Configure pull-down |
| Status LED (optional) | LED | GPIO 5 | 330 Ω resistor |

### Textual schematic notes

- **Power:** 5V 3A PSU to Pi. Servos powered from separate 5V 3–5A supply. Tie grounds together (Pi GND ↔ servo PSU GND).
- **Ultrasonic ECHO level shifting:** Use resistor divider (ECHO → 2 kΩ to GND, 1 kΩ to GPIO) to bring 5V down to ~3.3V.
- **Buzzer/actuators:** If buzzer draws >20 mA, drive via NPN transistor (e.g., 2N2222) with 1N4148/1N5819 flyback if inductive load.
- **EMI:** Route servo power separately, twist signal+ground to reduce noise near camera ribbons.
- **Cameras:** Fixed mounts; set exposure and white balance once to stabilize classification.

> Prior builds for smart bins highlight similar sensor stacks and a responsive web interface for lid control and bin monitoring, which parallels your module choices.

